1. Introduction:
Considering the severity of environmental deterioration and scarcity of energy storage systems, academia and industries devote prodigious research undertaking towards the development of promising and green sustainable energy storage devices. In this connection, supercapacitors (SCs) have been tremendously investigated as efficient energy storage devices for the next generation wearable electronics and hybrid vehicles [1]. Among other conventional devices, SCs are considered to be the most encouraging and suitable candidates for the growing energy demands of mankind owing to their distinctive features in terms of charge storage properties, power density, cyclic durability and charge discharge rate. Nevertheless, indigent energy output still remains the notable pitfall of SCs which needs a great improvement [2, 3]. Thus, current research trends deal with fabrication of hybrid SCs which constitute a battery-type electrode and a capacitor electrode to achieve enhanced electrochemical performance of SCs.
Various hybrid supercapacitors (HSCs) have been designed and explored as supercapacitor electrodes which endow remarkable results in terms of energy density, power output, operational voltage window and cycle life [4-9]. Additionally, the hybrid approach boosts the energy output of SCs due to the introduction of novel battery-type electrode materials having favorable attributes such as tunable structure, large specific surface area and excellent porosity [2, 4]. The main reason of high electrochemical performance of HSCs is their capability to acquire charge through electrostatic (electric-double layer formation) as well as electrochemical (Faradaic redox reactions) process [10].
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are the new class of crystalline materials formed via coordination covalent linkage of metal clusters and organic linker, having fascinating attributes such as tunable porosity, considerably large specific surface area and well-ordered crystal structure. Due to such properties, MOFs have been potentially exploited for various applications of electrochemical sensing [11, 12], catalysis [13], drug delivery [14], and chemical separation [15]. Over the past years, the application of MOFs [16-18] and MOF derived materials [19-21] as electrode materials has been extended for electrochemical energy storage systems. MOFs based on different metal centers such as Cu [22, 23] Fe [24], Co [25], Ni [26] and Zr [27] are reported in literature for supercapacitor applications. However, low electronic conductivity and poor stability in aqueous media are the major disadvantages of MOFs which hamper their practical applicability for supercapacitors. Hence, construction of novel electrode materials which will enhance the overall MOF performance is of paramount importance.
Recently, bimetallic MOFs are the attractive materials that have gathered considerable attention which involve two metal centers linked to an organic ligand. The involvement of two metal ions can effectively raise the electrochemical performance of the material via redox reactions of metal ions in the framework. For instance, Kazemi and co-workers [28] reported CoMn mixed metal MOF showed areal capacitance of $1.318\ \mathrm{F.cm^{-2}}$ . Also, bimetallic MOFs based on the combination of Ni-Co [29-31] and Co-Fe [32] metals have been studied for supercapacitive performance. Chen et al [33] fabricated dual MOFs using Co-Ni and Zn-Ni as the metal centers which delivered specific capacities of 236.1 and $161.5\ \mathrm{mA.h.g^{-1}}$ . Among the different mixed MOF electrodes, Cu and Fe based bimetallic MOF has not been investigated so far for supercapacitor application. Cu-1,3,5-benzene tricarboxylic acid (CuBTC) [22, 23] MOF when examined individually is found to exhibit specific capacitance of ${\sim}50$ and $85~\mathrm{F.g^{-1}}$ while FeBTC [24] shows low specific capacitance of ${\sim}57.5~\mathrm{F.g^{-1}}$ . The $\mathrm{Cu}$ and Fe metals have various advantages such as abundant availability, eco-friendliness and high chemical stability. Moreover, among various transition metals, Cu exhibits high conductivity and Fe shows different valence states and owns rich redox chemistry. Such properties make these metals suitable candidate for high performance supercapacitors and other electrochemical applications [34, 35]. Hence, in this study an attempt has been made to develop the electrode material combining Cu and Fe metal ions with BTC ligand (CuFeBTC) to explore its supercapacitive character. The charge storage in CuFeBTC relies upon the redox reactions of Cu and Fe metals in the framework, indicating its battery like behavior. Battery type electrodes are on the peak of current research progress due to their excellent charge storage capacity and high energy density though, their rate capability and cyclic durability are the important shortcomings of these materials, limiting their frequent use [36]. In this aspect, introduction of electrical double layer capacitive (EDLC) materials would interestingly be beneficial for high performance supercapacitive character of these materials. Particularly, heteroatom doped graphene has served as an extremely suitable candidate among different EDLC materials because alongwith inherent electrical conductivity and large surface area of graphene, loading of heteroatom in graphene lattice leads to accelerated charge density and wettability of electrode. Among various heteroatoms such as nitrogen [37, 38], sulphur [39- 41], and boron [42] doped graphene, incorporation of sulphur atom is preferential due to its high electronegativity which nearly resembles the electronegativity of carbon in graphitic sheets. Moreover, S doping significantly alter the spin densities on graphene thus furnishing considerably enhanced supercapacitive behavior [40, 41]. Also, the formation of C-S bonds at the edges creates a defective structure, availing substantial active sites which strongly impact the charging of electrical double layer ultimately expediting the pseudocapacitance.
The present study reports the synthesis of copper-iron 1,3,5-benzene tricarboxylic acid (CuFeBTC) based bimetallic MOF and its hybrid nanocomposite with S-doped graphene (S
GNS) using a facile wet chemical route. The individual addition Cu and Fe precursors during the reaction assured the uniform distribution of these metals in CuFeBTC. In CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite, S-GNS due to its high conductivity anchored the bimetallic MOF particles thereby acting as a nanoscale current collector. The as prepared CuFeBTC and CuFeBTC/S-GNS materials have been investigated as supercapacitor electrodes for the first time. The physicochemical aspects of CuFeBTC, S-GNS and CuFeBTC/S-GNS are thoroughly examined by various material characterization techniques such as powder X ray diffraction (XRD), Fourier transform infra-red spectroscopy (FT-IR), Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), Scanning electron microscopy (SEM), Transmission electron microscopy (TEM), energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis and Brunauer–Emmet– Teller (BET) analysis. Furthermore, electrochemical characterization of all the materials is performed by coating the active materials on the surface of glassy carbon electrode and their response is evaluated by employing cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostatic charge discharge (GCD) and impedance spectroscopy in 1 M $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ as an electrolyte. The electrochemical analysis reveals that CuFeBTC and CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite exhibit excellent supercapacitive behavior with high specific capacitance. A symmetric supercapacitor device using CuFeBTC/S-GNS hybrid nanocomposite has been constructed which delivers excellent energy storage capacity and greater power output with remarkable cycle life. Thus, it is anticipated that the designed CuFeBTC/S-GNS hybrid nanocomposite would serve as a promising material for various energy related applications.
2. Experimental section:
2.1 Synthetic procedures:
2.1.1 Synthesis of copper-iron 1,3,5 benzene tricarboxylic acid (CuFeBTC):
All the chemicals and reagents of analytical grade were acquired from Aldrich and used as received. The synthesis of CuFeBTC nanopowder was carried out by following the procedure reported elsewhere [43] with certain modifications. Briefly, 5.0 mmol Trimesic acid (1, 3, 5- benzene tricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ ) was dissolved in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of ethanol/water $\protect\mathrm{(v/v^{\circ}=1/1}\protect)$ ) solvent under constant stirring conditions. In this solution, equimolar concentrations of $25~\mathrm{mL}$ 2.0 mmol $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ each metal precursors were added simultaneously till a thick bluish green colour precipitate was observed. Successively, the above solution was magnetically stirred for 20 minutes at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ until homogeneity was obtained. The reaction was continued for one hour followed by aging for 3 hours. The magnetic stirring for the reaction was performed at a speed of 600 rpm. Subsequently, the product was isolated by filtration using a Millipore vacuum filtration assembly where the product was collected on Millipore filter paper. After filtration was complete the product was washed thoroughly with ethanol and was left to dry in a vacuum oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 6 hours.
2.1.2 Synthesis of sulphur doped graphene (S-GNS):
Initially, graphene oxide was synthesized from graphite powder as per the procedure reported in literature [44]. Then, graphene oxide was converted to graphene using glucose as a green reducing agent. In a typical synthesis, graphene oxide was dispersed in distilled water and 60 mg of glucose was added to this solution under constant stirring for one hour. Further, $25\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of ammonia was introduced in the above solution and reaction was continued for 2 hours at 100 $^\mathrm{{{\circ}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ . The obtained black powder was separated by centrifugation and the residue was dried in oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 4 hours.
During the synthesis of S-GNS, the synthetic procedure reported in literature [45] was followed with minor changes in the reported synthesis. The as-prepared graphene was redispersed in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ distilled water using an ultrasonic processor. Further, this solution was transferred to a round bottom flask and ${\sim}4\ \mathrm{g}$ of sodium thiosulphate $(0.25~\mathrm{M})$ in 20 mL 1 M ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ was added. This was refluxed at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 hours. Eventually, the product was separated using a centrifuge at $5000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for 15 minutes and washed using ethanol and distilled water followed by drying at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 6 hours.
2.1.3 Synthesis of CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite:
The synthesis of CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite was performed following the similar procedure of CuFeBTC. Firstly, S-GNS and trimesic acid were dispersed in $100~\mathrm{mL}$ distilled water/ethanol mixture with the aid of ultrasonication and to this solution, the equimolar concentrations of Cu and Fe metal salts were added under magnetic stirring. Thereafter, the above synthetic procedure was followed and the final product was collected by filtration. The residue was subjected to several washings of ethanol and dried in vacuum oven at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 6 hours and labelled as CuFeBTC/S-GNS. The schematic representation of synthesis of hybrid nanocomposite is illustrated in Scheme 1.
