2.2 Material characterization:
2.2.1 Structural investigation:
2.2.2.1 X-ray diffraction, FT-IR spectroscopy and Thermogravimetry analysis:
X ray diffraction study was performed on X ray diffractometer (Shimadzu 7000S, Shimadzu Analytical, Japan) equipped with CuKα radiation $(\lambda{=}0.154~\mathrm{nm}$ ) in the 2θ range of $10^{\circ}$ to $80^{\circ}$ in order to understand the crystallographic phases of as prepared materials. Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy was carried out to identify the characteristic absorption bands present in materials and the spectra were recorded on FT-IR spectrometer model (3000 Hyperion Microscope with Vertex 80 FTIR System) from Bruker, Germany. Thermogravimetry analysis was performed using Perkin Elmer analyzer (Model: Pyris Diamond) to examine the thermal character of as-prepared materials.
2.2.2 Surface characterization:
2.2.2.1 Morphological studies:
The morphology of the synthesized materials was analysed using field emission scanning electron microscopy conducted on FEG-SEM JEOL microscope (Model JSM 7600F; operating voltage ${\mathrm{\Omega}}=15{\mathrm{~kV}}.$ ). The detailed microstructure was studied using transmission electron microscope (FEI Tecnai G2, F30 with an operating voltage of $300\mathrm{kV}$ and resolution of 2.0 Å) by recording TEM micrographs.
2.2.2.2 Elemental Survey:
The chemical composition of each of the elements present in the CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite was investigated by energy dispersive X ray spectroscopy where various element maps have been recorded. Additionally, X ray photoelectron spectroscopy was also employed (Model: PHI 5000 Versa Probe II, FEI Inc.) to determine quantitative element composition alongwith their electronic states and binding energies.
2.2.2.3 Surface area and pore character:
The determination of surface area and pore volume of all the materials was performed by measuring $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms at $77~\mathrm{{K}}$ on a BET surface analyzer (Quanta chrome Autosorb 1C). Before beginning the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption, the samples were degassed at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for 2 hours prior to each measurement. Brunauer–Emmet–Teller (BET) method was utilized to calculate the surface area and pore volume while the pore diameter was calculated using BJH equation.
2.3 Electrochemical characterization:
The supercapacitive character of all the prepared materials was investigated by performing electrochemical experiments on Autolab PGSTAT 302 work station equipped with
USB interface controlled with NOVA software (version 1.11.2) in 1 M $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ neutral electrolyte utilizing both three and two electrode cells. The electrochemical behavior of CuFeBTC, S-GNS and CuFeBTC/S-GNS materials was studied employing various techniques viz. cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostatic charge/discharge (GCD) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy.
2.3.1 Three electrode system:
The three electrode cell was assembled using a glassy carbon electrode (GCE) as a working electrode, $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ (Sat. KCl) as a reference electrode and Pt wire as an auxiliary electrode in 1 M $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ as a neutral electrolyte. Initially, the dispersion of all active materials was prepared where $6~\mathrm{mg}$ of the materials were uniformly dispersed in $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF solution using an ultrasonic processor for 15 minutes. The maximum power and frequency used for ultrasonication was 120 W and $40~\mathrm{{kHz}}$ respectively. Thereafter, to fabricate the working electrode, $20~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of this dispersion was drop casted on the surface of GCE. The working potential range of -0.4 to $+1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ was used to perform CV and GCD measurements while the impedance analysis was conducted in the frequency range of 0.1 to ${10^{6}\ \mathrm{Hz}}$ with a voltage amplitude of 5 mV.
2.3.2 Two electrode system:
In the two electrode system, the homogeneous paste of CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite (90 wt. $\%$ ), acetylene black $(5\mathrm{\textrm{wt.\%}})$ and poly (vinylidene fluoride) $(5\mathrm{\textrm{wt.\%}})$ in $N-$ methyl pyrrolidone as a solvent was prepared. Further, a carbon paper was cut into small disc like pieces and the above mixture $(\sim2~\mathrm{mg})$ was incorporated on its surface. These loaded disc shape pieces were kept for drying overnight in a vacuum oven at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The symmetric supercapacitor was fabricated by using two of the above similar pieces of carbon paper and used as a working electrode. A cellulosic separator soaked with 1 M ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ electrolyte was inserted in between two electrodes before the measurement and the entire set up of two electrode system was assembled in a Swagelok cell. The cyclic voltammograms were recorded at different potentials bearing a wide working potential window of -0.4 to $+1.4\mathrm{~V~}$ and GCD curves at various current densities from 1.0 to $20.0\mathrm{A.g^{-1}}$ were obtained.
2.3.3 Calculation of electrochemical parameters:
The following equation was utilized to calculate the specific capacitance $\displaystyle(\ensuremath{\mathrm{C_{sp}}})$ of all the active materials from GCD studies [25, 46]:
$$
\mathbf{C_{sp}}\left(\mathbf{F.g^{-1}}\right)=\frac{\mathbf{I.}\Delta\mathbf{t}}{\Delta\mathbf{V.m}}\qquad\ldots\left(1\right)
$$
where, I is the discharge current (A), Δt is the discharge time (s), m is mass of active material (g) on the electrode, $\Delta\mathrm{V}$ is the working potential window (V).
