3. Results and Discussion:
3.1 Material characterization:
3.1.1 Structural investigation using XRD, FT-IR and TGA:
The crystallographic aspects and phase purity of as prepared materials viz. CuFeBTC, SGNS and CuFeBTC/S-GNS composite were examined by X ray diffraction analysis and the respective diffraction patterns obtained from this study are represented in Fig. S1(A) (Supporting Information). The XRD pattern of CuFeBTC shows many sharp diffraction peaks in the 2θ region of $15^{\mathrm{{o}}}$ to $45^{\mathrm{{o}}}$ which indicate the polycrystalline nature of CuFeBTC. The peaks in this region can be indexed to the cubic crystalline phase of copper trimesate trihydrate in the bimetallic MOF [22, 23]. Comparably, FeBTC is found to exhibit a low order of crystallinity [47] hence, when combined with Cu using BTC ligand, the crystallinity of Fe tends to improve. Thus, in CuFeBTC distinct diffraction peaks are visible which are relative to the presence of both Cu and Fe metals in CuFeBTC. The absence of additional peaks for any impurities or other residues imparts the high phase purity of the as prepared sample. The diffraction peaks around 2θ $=24.1^{\circ}$ and $43.3^{\mathrm{o}}$ in S-GNS indicate that the graphene sheets are well exfoliated. Also, these broad and weak intense peaks suggest that there is the formation of defects or vacancies on the graphene layers due to the heteroatom doping [48, 49]. It is observed from the XRD pattern of CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite that the sharpness and intensity of peaks of CuFeBTC slightly increases due to the orderly existence of S-GNS. However, the diffraction peaks corresponding to S-GNS are not clearly identified in the nanocomposite since they get shielded by the polycrystalline CuFeBTC particles. Hence, XRD studies ensured the successful formation of CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite.
FT-IR spectroscopy was carried out in order to obtain the valuable information regarding the functional groups and chemical bonds present in the materials. The recorded IR spectra of CuFeBTC, S-GNS and CuFeBTC/S-GNS are shown in Fig. S1(B) (Supporting Information). In the IR spectrum of CuFeBTC, the formation of Cu and Fe with the same building block that is the BTC anion is visible from the presence of characteristic absorption bands below ${\sim}2000~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ . Specifically, the bands appearing in the zone of 1300-1700 cm-1 can be assigned to the symmetric and asymmetric stretching vibration modes of carboxylate groups in the organic ligand. In addition, the broad band observed at ${\sim}3400~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ can be assigned to -OH stretching and bending modes of feeble water molecules in the porous bimetallic MOF [50, 51]. It is evident from the spectrum of S-GNS that the absorption peak ${\sim}1150~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ corresponds to the symmetrical stretching of C-S bond while the small band at ${\sim}1352~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ indicates the presence of sulfonyl group in the graphene. Moreover, the absorption band observed near ${\sim}1600~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ can be indexed to the appearance of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ bond in the S-GNS. Also, due to the sulphur heteroatom doping, the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ vibrations falling in the range of \~1700-1720cm-1 are absent suggesting the deoxygenation of carbonyl groups [49, 52]. In CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite, the prominent absorption bands noticeable below ${\sim}2000~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ can be attributed to the existence of CuFeBTC while the distinguishable precise peak at ${\sim}1370~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ certifies the presence of S-GNS in the CuFeBTC/SGNS nanocomposite. Therefore, the FT-IR spectroscopic analysis not only reveals the formation of the hybrid nanocomposite but also proves the heteroatom doping of S on the exfoliated graphene layers.
Thermogravimetric studies were carried out in order to investigate the thermal stability of CuFeBTC, S-GNS materials and CuFeBTC/SGNS composite. The relevant information concerning the chemical character and weight percentage of all the materials was gained from this analysis. Fig. S1(C) (Supporting Information) presents the TGA curves recorded for all the samples. Apparently, CuFeBTC exhibits maximum weight loss with subsequent steps when pyrolyzed to greater temperature. The first loss of ${\sim}25\%$ at $280~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ is observed for CuFeBTC which can be ascribed to loss of weakly bonded water molecules in the bimetallic MOF. The second and third step weight loss in the temperature range of 360 to $435~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ demonstrates the calcination of organic moiety present in CuFeBTC framework. The significant loss in weight $(\sim72\%$ total) is observable at the temperature of ${\sim}760~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ which implies the complete thermal degradation of the bimetallic MOF material. The thermogram of S-GNS manifests maximum thermal resistance of the heteroatom doped graphene nanosheets up to high temperature of 750 $^\mathrm{{{\circ}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ beyond which it exhibits a negligible weight loss of $4\%.$ The thermal behavior of CuFeBTC is found to improve in CuFeBTC/S-GNS due to the introduction of S-GNS since the weight loss observed for CuFeBTC in the nanocomposite is reduced as compared to pristine CuFeBTC. However, the first step pyrolysis indicates the burning of organic ligand in the bimetallic MOF whereas the next step loss displays the weight loss of $45\%$ at the temperature of ${\sim}700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Hence, it can be concluded from the TGA analysis that the incorporation of S-GNS in the bimetallic MOF effectively raises the thermal stability of CuFeBTC thereby soothing its weight loss.
3.1.2 Surface Characterization:
3.1.2.1 Morphological Studies:
The morphological features of the prepared samples viz. CuFeBTC, S-GNS and CuFeBTC/S-GNS were examined by performing SEM analysis. The micrographs of these materials recorded at different resolutions from this study are outlined in Fig. 1(A-F) It is observed from Fig. 1(A) and (B) that CuFeBTC depicts a mixed morphology of regular cube shaped structures along with some irregular assorted particles. Here, the uniform and large nanocubes can be attributed to the existence of Cu while the agglomerated particles present alongside and on the cubes are recognized as the Fe functionalities in the bimetallic MOF. Also, from this micrograph the CuFeBTC structure is observed to be porous in nature. This result is in good agreement with the reported literature for CuBTC and FeBTC [53]. The SEM images of SGNS are depicted in Fig. 1(C) and (D) where numerous exfoliated graphene sheets are intertwined. It is seen from the figure that the graphene sheets are substantially crumpled due to the heteroatom doping of S atom thereby resulting in formation of a defective structure. Such structural conformation extremely favors the adsorption of CuFeBTC particles on the surface of S-GNS. Fig. 1(E) and (F) shows the micrograph of CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite where the appearance of both the individual components is distinctly visible. Moreover, the CuFeBTC particles are uniformly decorated on the surface of S-GNS where the bimetallic MOF act as spacer, thus preventing the restacking of graphene sheets in the hybrid nanocomposite.
The detailed characteristic microstructure of the materials was investigated by conducting TEM and the micrographs recorded are depicted in Fig. 1(G-L). The TEM image of CuFeBTC (Fig. 1(G) and (H)) displays regular presence of big nanocubes and tiny agglomerated globular clusters. The magnified micrograph shows the sheer cubic morphology of the CuBTC which is adjoined and wrapped by the FeBTC clusters in CuFeBTC material. Fig. 1(I) and (J) illustrate the TEM micrograph of S-GNS where an assembly of various graphene sheets is observed which trait immense wrinkles on their surface. This feature implies that the surface of graphene has been successfully modified with the sulphur atom. In CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite, the discrete attributes of CuFeBTC and S-GNS are well exhibited where the bulky CuFeBTC particles are regularly arrayed on the surface of S-GNS (Fig. 1(K) and (L)). Furthermore, the high resolution TEM analysis of CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite was also carried out and the image obtained from this study is represented in Fig. S2(A) (Supporting Information). It is worth noting from the HRTEM micrograph that both the materials in the nanocomposite outline their independent existence where the absence of lattice fringes indicates that the amount of amorphous FeBTC (consistent with XRD results) contributing from the CuFeBTC framework is in large amount and the carbonaceous S-GNS reduces the crystallinity of the pristine CuFeBTC (as observed in the electron diffraction pattern of the nanocomposite (Fig. S2(B)); Supporting Information). Also, the planar lattice of graphene in the HRTEM is identified which suggests that the doped graphene sheets remain structurally united without any defects in the CuFeBTC/SGNS nanocomposite. Hence, the morphological examination reveals that the nanomaterials have been successfully formed and CuFeBTC has been integrated over the doped graphene sheets.
