Furthermore, X ray photoelectron spectroscopy was employed to validate the speculation of the complementary information obtained from EDS analysis and understand the surface states along with nature of chemical bonding of all the elements present in the nanocomposite. The results of XPS analysis are illustrated in Fig. 3. Fig. 3(A) unveils the wide survey spectrum recorded for CuFeBTC/S-GNS which shows distinct peaks at different binding energies in the range of 50 to $1000{\mathrm{~eV}}$ which are relative to the signals of C 1s, O 1s, S 2p, Cu 2p and Fe 2p elemental states respectively. The high-resolution C 1s spectrum can be deconvoluted into five peaks where the peaks at 284.6 and $286.6~\mathrm{eV}$ represent the non-oxygenated carbon functional groups viz. $C{=}C$ , C-C and C-H corresponding to the $\displaystyle\mathbf{sp}^{2}$ and sp3 hybridized C atom. Additionally, the peaks at binding energy levels 287.9, 286.8 and $288.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be indexed to carbon in C-S bond, epoxy carbon and carbonyl carbon. The C-S peak suggests the existence of S atom covalently bonded to graphene in its lattice structure thus confirming the incorporation of S heteroatom in the exfoliated graphene layers (Fig. 3(B)) [49, 54]. The O 1s spectrum is fitted to three peaks at the binding energies of 531.2, 532.3 and $537.8~\mathrm{eV}$ where the first two prominent peaks can be imputed to the metal oxygen bonding (Cu-O) and (Fe-O) in the bimetallic MOF while the third peak imparts -OH groups of the surface adsorbed water molecules in the nanocomposite. Also, the peak relative to $\mathrm{O=}\mathsf{S}$ bond is noticeable at the binding energy of 531.6 eV which certifies the regular presence of S containing groups [47, 54] (Fig. 3(C)). The core level S 2p spectrum is represented in Fig. 3(D) where the deconvoluted peaks at the binding energies of 163.7 and $164.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ are observed which can be assigned to the bonding interactions of aromatic sulfide (C-S-C and - $\mathrm{\cdotC=S\rangle}$ ) while the peak at $168.4\mathrm{eV}$ can be ascribed to the existence of oxidized sulphur moieties [49, 54]. The Cu 2p and Fe 2p spectra are presented in Fig. 3(E) and (F) where in Cu 2p spectrum, two distinctive peaks at respective binding energies of 932.4 and $952.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ are observed corresponding to the $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ levels. Moreover, the satellite peak \~939.8 eV is also visible which implies that Cu ions are divalent. Also, the fitted Fe 2p spectrum depicts two peaks at the binding energies 711.9 and 725.6 eV suggesting the occurrence of Fe $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ and Fe $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ levels revealing the Fe (III) state in the material. Thus, the chemical bonding and surface states of the elements achieved from XPS analysis evidently corroborate the formation of bimetallic MOF and CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite.
3.1.2.3 Determination of Surface area and pore size:
The surface area and pore character are the crucial determinants of the electroactive materials which are used for electrochemical energy storage processes. The surface area and pore size distribution of CuBTC and CuFeBTC/S-GNS were calculated by conducting $\Nu_{2}$ adsorptiondesorption experiments. The isotherms obtained from this measurement are presented in Fig. 4 where CuFeBTC and CuFeBTC/S-GNS both exhibit typical type IV $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms. Also, these isotherms outline distinct hysteresis loops which stipulate the mesoporous character of as-prepared materials. The Brauner Emmett Teller (BET) specific surface areas of CuFeBTC and CuFeBTC/S-GNS are calculated to be 313.54 and $568.75\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ with large the pore volume of 0.24 and $0.52~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ respectively. The addition of S-GNS constructively upraises the surface area of pristine CuFeBTC thereby resulting in elevation of its electrochemical charge storage. The pore size of these materials was also determined from the BJH plots shown inset of Fig. 4 as (A) and (B) where the maxima peaks display the pore size of 16.80 and $9.71\ \mathrm{nm}$ for CuFeBTC and CuFeBTC/S-GNS respectively. Therefore, the BET analysis depicts the absolute mesoporous aspect of the prepared materials. The porosity of the material has an immediate relation with the electrochemical performance however, in CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite, the large porosity of CuFeBTC facilitates the transfer of ions at the electrode/solution interface thus reducing any volume change during the charge-discharge process due to rigid structure of BTC ligand. This result is in accordance with the reported literature [30].
3.2 Electrochemical characterization:
The electrochemical performance of as-prepared samples was first investigated in a threeelectrode configuration where the glassy carbon electrode coated with the active materials viz. CuFeBTC, S-GNS and CuFeBTC/S-GNS was used as a working electrode, Ag/AgCl as a reference electrode and a platinum wire as a counter electrode. The electrochemical measurements were carried out in a working potential range of -0.4 to $+1.0\mathrm{,V}$ in a neutral 1 M $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ electrolyte. Initially, the behavior of all the samples was examined in different solutions of alkaline and neutral electrolytes such as 3 M KOH, 1 M $\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and 1 M $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ employing cyclic voltammetry (Fig. S3; Supporting Information). The acidic electrolytes were not used in the study as MOFs get readily dissolved in the acidic media. It is observed from the voltammograms that CuFeBTC exhibits low electrochemical response in 3 M KOH because of its poor stability. On the other hand, when $1\mathrm{MK}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and 1 M $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ electrolytes are used for the analysis, the capacitive behavior of the bimetallic CuFeBTC MOF is significantly enhanced. However, the best electrochemical behavior of CuFeBTC is observed in 1 M $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ due to smaller ionic radius of $\mathrm{Na}^{+}(0.95\mathrm{\AA})$ than $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ (1.33 Å) which readily enables the penetration of $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ cations in the porous CuFeBTC framework resulting in large current as compared to $1\textbf{M}$ ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ [24, 55]. Thus, it can be concluded from the figure that bimetallic CuFeBTC MOF displays highest electrochemical activity in 1 M $\mathrm{[Na_{2}S O_{4}}$ electrolyte.
Fig. 5(A) depicts the cyclic voltammograms recorded for three materials viz., CuFeBTC, SGNS and CuFeBTC/S-GNS hybrid nanocomposite over the potential range of -0.4 to $1.0\mathrm{V}$ . In general, the charge storage mechanism taking place at the electrode surface can be categorized in to three types: EDLC and/or surface pseudocapacitive, Pseudocapacitance due to intercalation with partial redox reactions and Faradaic and/or battery like [56]. The CV curve of CuFeBTC indicates that the charge storage occurs due to the $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ ion intercalation/de-intercalation through the porous CuFeBTC structure along with partial redox reactions of the Cu and Fe $[\mathrm{II}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{III}\rangle$ metals with $\mathrm{SO}_{4}{}^{2-}$ ions during the anodic and cathodic sweep [57]. The CV profile of S-GNS manifests an ideal rectangular shape of the voltammogram, suggesting the formation of electrical double layer. In contrast, the CuFeBTC/S-GNS hybrid nanocomposite exhibits a large integrated area under the CV curve with nearly rectangular shape, suggesting high charge storage ability. This can be attributed to the synergy of bimetallic MOF and S-GNS which avails the combination of pseudocapacitance originating from surface redox processes of metals and EDLC charge storage mechanism offered by S-GNS. This is due to regular presence of S-GNS (consistent with morphological studies) which shows the dominant EDLC behavior during the charge storage and in turn boosts the electronic conductivity of bimetallic CuFeBTC in the composite material. Thus, it is evident from this curve that the hybrid nanocomposite demonstrates an elevated supercapacitive performance. The schematic illustration of the charge storage mechanism is outlined in Scheme 2.
Fig. 5(B) and (C) display the CV profiles of CuFeBTC nanopowder and CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite electrodes measured to study the effect of scan rate from 5 to $500\ \mathrm{mV}.\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . It is evident from Fig. 5(B) that CuFeBTC shows pair of redox peaks even at high scan rate suggesting its excellent electrochemical reversibility and rate performance whereas in case of CuFeBTC/S-GNS (Fig. 5(C)), with the increasing scan rate, the supercapacitive character of composite is found to improve due to steep rise in the specific current. This improvement is a result of the addition of S-GNS in the CuFeBTC framework which offers maximum EDLC effect to the pristine CuFeBTC thereby promoting its electrochemical performance. Furthermore, impedance analysis of the electrodes was also performed and the Nyquist plots obtained for CuFeBTC, S-GNS and CuFeBTC/S-GNS by fitting the electrochemical impedance data in the circuit are outlined in Fig. 5(D). The Nyquist plot consists of two regions: (a) semicircular portion in high frequency which delineates the charge transfer component and (b) a straight line in low frequency region is associated with diffusion limited process occurring during the electrochemical charge storage. The diameter of the semi-circular region in the Nyquist plot represents the charge transfer resistance $\mathrm{(R_{ct})}$ which is related with the interface between electrode material and electrolyte and current collector. The fitted $\mathrm{R_{ct}}$ values obtained for CuFeBTC, S-GNS and CuFeBTC/S-GNS are 3.18, 1.05 and $0.91~\Omega$ respectively. It is worth noting that the hybrid nanocomposite has the lowest charge transfer resistance as compared to the pristine CuFeBTC and S-GNS. Hence, impedance study reveals that in CuFeBTC/S-GNS composite rapid electron and electrolyte ion transfer takes place across its structure implying that the composite electrode offers maximum electrochemical charge storage capability among the other electrodes.
CuFeBTC, S-GNS and CuFeBTC/S-GNS coated on GCE at $10\ \mathrm{mV}.\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ ; Scan rate studies of (B) CuFeBTC; (C) CuFeBTC/S-GNS by varying scan rate from 5 to $500\ \mathrm{mV}.\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and (D) Nyquist plots of CuFeBTC, S-GNS and CuFeBTC/S-GNS recorded from impedance analysis (inset: equivalent circuit used for data fitting) in 1 M $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ electrolyte
After understanding the capacitive behavior of the prepared materials by CV studies, the electrochemical scrutiny was further extended to evaluate the charge discharge ability of the materials. This was carried out by conducting galvanostatic charge discharge (GCD) experiments for CuFeBTC, S-GNS and CuFeBTC/S-GNS and the GCD curves obtained from this experiment are depicted in Fig. 6(A). In CuFeBTC, it is noticeable that the charging and discharging curves are non-linear which indicate that the charge is stored in CuFeBTC mainly via the faradaic redox reactions of Cu and Fe metals (in accordance with the CV behavior) whereas the S-GNS shows an ideal symmetrical GCD curve. The GCD profile of CuFeBTC/S-GNS hybrid nanocomposite notifies closely a linear shape suggesting that the integration of S-GNS on the bimetallic MOF not only offers high electrical conductivity but also enhances the overall charging-discharging time analogous to other materials. Thus, GCD analysis ensures the expedited supercapacitive behavior of CuFeBTC when combined with the S-GNS. Further, the GCD curves of CuFeBTC and CuFeBTC/S-GNS recorded at various current densities from 0.5 to $5.0\mathrm{A.g^{-1}}$ are presented in Fig. 6(B) and (C).  This data in equation (1) has been used to compute the specific capacitances of CuFeBTC and CuFeBTC/S-GNS hybrid composite. The value of specific capacitance for CuFeBTC/S-GNS at various current densities of 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0 and $5.0\ \mathrm{A.g^{-1}}$ are calculated to be 1164.3, 1087.2, 1004.3, 964.5, 938.9, 912.5 F.g-1 respectively. It is observed from Fig. 6(D) that the specific capacitances moderately diminish when plotted as a function of current density from 0.5 to 5.0 A.g-1. The most probable reason associated with this trend is the natural increase in concentration polarization [58]. In addition, the hybrid composite retains $78.4\%$ of its initial specific capacitance even at high current density of $5.0~\mathrm{A.g^{-1}}$ analogous to pristine CuFeBTC which preserves ${\sim}69\%$ of its original capacitance. Hence, it is apparent that the hybrid nanocomposite displays good rate capability (Fig. 6(E)). In general, the synergistic effect between the three aspects viz. structure, morphology, and conductivity of the electrode materials is overall accountable for their high electrochemical performance. The following facts can be assigned for the high specific capacitance of hybrid composite: (i) the distinctive structure of CuFeBTC/S-GNS hybrid nanocomposite permits the immediate contact between large surface area S-GNS sheets and CuFeBTC network; (ii) the porous bimetallic CuFeBTC renders high charge storage while S-GNS protects the bimetallic MOF from structural damage; (iii) the pervaded S-GNS sheets anchors the individual CuFeBTC particles and this attachment raises the conductivity thereby leading to effective charge transport across the structure and (iv) S-GNS offers high electrical double layer charge storage by adsorption of electrolyte ions on its surface. Such electrochemical performance of the material is the consequence of tunable porous structure of bimetallic MOF accumulated on the surface of S-GNS, offering electronic conductivity and large specific surface area. This interconnected structure enables swift electron and electrolyte transport during the charge-discharge process furnishing an improvement in the electrochemical kinetics. For a comparative study, specific capacitance of CuFeBTC/S-GNS has been compared with the materials of similar class including single and bimetallic MOFs which has been presented as Table S1 in supporting information.
The cycle life of CuFeBTC and CuFeBTC/S-GNS electrodes was investigated by recording successive GCD cycles at $0.5\mathrm{A.g^{-1}}$ in three electrode system and the plot of specific capacitance versus cycle number is shown in Fig. 6(F). It is observed from the figure that CuFeBTC/S-GNS composite evinces $90.2\%$ of its initial specific capacitance retention whereas bare CuFeBTC maintains $79.4\%$ of its original specific capacitance after 4500 potential cycles. It is obvious that the incorporated S-GNS provides structural stability to bimetallic MOF preventing CuFeBTC from corrosion or volumetric shrinks originating from the faradaic redox reactions of the metals. Therefore, it can be concluded that the hybrid composite shows durable cycling performance and is capable to withstand repetitive charge discharge cycles. As evidence to this study, TEM and EIS analysis of CuFeBTC/S-GNS composite after 4500 cycles was performed (Fig. S4 (Supporting Information)). These studies confirm that the hybrid composite is structurally stable.
