3.3 Application of CuFeBTC/S-GNS hybrid composite in symmetric supercapacitor:
The practical applicability of the hybrid composite was assessed by exploring its electrochemical activity in two electrode system where CuFeBTC/S-GNS nanocomposite was used as anode and cathode and a symmetric supercapacitor (SS) device using these electrodes in a Swagelok cell was configured. Initially, CV experiments were carried out at different voltages to optimize a fixed potential for the SS device. It is observed from Fig. 7(A) that the SS device gives stable response till the potential 1.8 V beyond which certain unwanted irreversible reactions occur resulting in rapid increase of the specific current. The other reason which can account for this observation is the decomposition of electrolyte beyond $1.8{\mathrm{V}}$ thus, this potential was selected as the operational voltage for all further experiments. The CV curves display nearly an ideal rectangular shape with a slight arch in the anodic and cathodic region. This nature of the curve certifies the pseudo-constant rate of charging and discharging of the assembled SS device over the entire CV range. Similarly, charge discharge studies were also performed at various potentials (Fig. 7(B)) where it is found that $1.8\mathrm{V}$ exhibits highest charging and discharging time as compared to the other recorded GCD curves. Furthermore, the influence of scan rate was also examined on the cyclic voltammetric behavior of SS device by varying the scan rate from 10 to $500\ \mathrm{mV}.\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ (Fig. 7(C)). It is evident from the scan rate study that the CV curves continue to maintain their ideal shape even at high scan rates indicating the ideal behavior and excellent reversibility of the SS. Fig. 7(D) outlines the charge discharge cycles of the fabricated SS device recorded at different current densities in the range of 1.0 to $20.0\mathrm{A.g^{-1}}$ . It is noteworthy from this plot that the SS exhibits symmetrical charge discharge profiles at all current densities which signifies good rate capability and high electrochemical stability of the SS device. Additionally, cyclic durability was evaluated since it is one of the most imperative factors for investigating the capacitive performance of the device. It is apparent from Fig. 7(E) that assembled CuFeBTC/SGNS SS manifests a long-term cycle life till 10000 consecutive cycles along with greater coulombic efficiency of ${\sim}99\%$ suggesting excellent cyclic stability. The SS also shows $92.5\%$ of its initial specific capacitance retention which reveals that the assembled CuFeBTC/S-GNS symmetric supercapacitor possesses high potential for repetitive charge discharge cycles (Fig. 7(F)). Furthermore, the two main assessment parameters of supercapacitor viz. energy (E) and power (P) density were determined using equations (2) and (3). The CuFeBTC/S-GNS SS delivers high energy output of 96.57 Wh.kg-1 and power density of $1545.12\mathrm{W.kg^{-1}}$ at the current density of $1.0~\mathrm{A.g^{-1}}$ and even when the current density is increased to $20.0~\mathrm{A.g^{-1}}$ , the SS still shows fairly good energy storage of $50.2~\mathrm{Wh.kg^{-1}}$ . A Ragone plot giving the relation of energy density as a function of power density is depicted in Fig. 8 where the comparison of E and P of the constructed SS with the previously reported bimetallic MOFs is presented. Table 1 shows a comparison of the assembled CuFeBTC SS device with the similar class of bimetallic MOFs and single MOF based supercapacitors. It is noticeable that the proposed SS device offers high energy density and extended voltage among other asymmetric and symmetric devices reported in literature.
