4. Conclusion:
The performance of CuFeBTC/S-GNS hybrid nanocomposite for supercapacitor application has been investigated. The material characterization study reveals that the bimetallic CuFeBTC MOF particles have been successfully integrated on the surface of S-GNS. The individual features of bimetallic MOF and heteroatom doped graphene such as tunable porosity, considerably large specific surface area and high electrical conductivity significantly enhance the overall electrochemical performance of this hybrid composite. In particular, the extreme porous and hollow structure of the material renders adequate channels for electrolyte ion transfer thus elevating the charge transport across the electrode/electrolyte interface. Also, the presence of active redox sites in the bimetallic MOF along with the superior conductivity of S-GNS in the nanocomposite readily enables the penetration of electrolyte ions in the active material which results in high specific capacitance. The fabricated symmetric supercapacitor has an operational voltage of $1.8\mathrm{~V~}$ where it exhibits an excellent cycle life of 10000 cycles and retains ${\sim}92.5\%$ of its original specific capacitance at a current density of $1.0\mathrm{A.g^{-1}}$ . Hence, it is envisaged from the above findings that CuFeBTC/S-GNS hybrid nanocomposite would serve as a constructive tool for advanced energy storage systems.
